\begin{document}
\title{Fast loops with offarrays}
\author{Mark Bravington; updated Nov 2026}

\maketitle

<<setup,cache=FALSE, echo=TRUE, include=FALSE>>=
#debug::debug_knitr( mtrace=FALSE)
@

The \texttt{offarray} package lets you work with arrays (and matrices
and vectors) whose indexing can start wherever you like--- not just
at 1 as in base-R, nor just at 0 as in misbegotten languages like
C. This is great for applications like population dynamics: you can
start your years at 2015, you can start your range of ages at 8, etc.
Simpler, clearer, less buggity-bug-bug.

New features in \texttt{offarray} v2.0.74 are: \texttt{autobayes}
(to reduce 3 steps to 1), and auto-clamping of array indices inside
\texttt{autoloop} via e.g. \texttt{x{[}\textasciitilde i+j{]}} to
avoid OOB accesses (i.e., in this case to limit \texttt{i+j} to the
range of allowed indices for the first dimension of \texttt{x}).

Most R-ish things work as you would expect on \texttt{offarray} objects.
There is documentation! Try \texttt{?offarray} (where exceptions are
noted) and \texttt{utils::example('offarray','offarray')}.

<<intro>>=
library( mvbutils)
library( offarray)
options( digits=4)
bloop <- offarray( 1:6, dimseq=list( 11:12, 21:23))
bloop + 3
bloop > 0
bloop[11,]
bloop[SLICE=11,] # drops the dimension (drop is off by default)
## utils::example( 'offarray', 'offarray')...
## ... or even try reading the documentation of '?offarray'...
@

You can also use \texttt{offarray} with Kasper Kristensen's stupendous\texttt{
RTMB} package--- so you can get first derivatives in high-dimensional
problems really fast, and fit complicated statistical models with
remarkable ease. (Of course, the whole point of \texttt{RTMB} is that
you can use \textquotedbl regular\textquotedbl{} R code. But it took
some work to get \texttt{offarray} working with it.) \textbf{This
is amazing!!!}

\subsection{Package \texttt{TMBO}}

Note that you can also use \texttt{offarray} with my package \texttt{TMBO},
which deliberately lets you write flexible-offset TMB code (in C++,
of course; that's good old \textquotedbl TMB\textquotedbl , not
\textquotedbl\texttt{RTMB}\textquotedbl ) that is fully \texttt{offarray}-compatible
on the R side. In marked contrast to \textquotedbl native TMB\textquotedbl ,
TMBO also gives very informative non-crashy error messages when you
go Out-Of-Bounds (OOB) or commit other array-access stuff-ups, which
are by far the commonest problem. However, I suspect that TMBO ---
which was a lot of work to write in late 2023, and which I'm kinda
proud of--- will be obsolete almost immediately, because \texttt{RTMB}
and \texttt{offarray} will just remove the need ever to go near C++
again \footnote{Though, speed remains a \emph{possible} concern with the \texttt{MakeADFun}
step in \texttt{RTMB}}. And I will shed no tears whatsoever if that is the case.

\section{Loops and speed}

When you need to to fill in an offarray one-element-at-a-time, for-loops
are going to be slow, especially nested ones. A staggering amount
of argument-checking gets done behind the scenes in R\emph{ every
time} a single offarray element is read; and every time one element
is changed, R makes a \textquotedbl deep copy\textquotedbl\footnote{This deep-copy-on-subassignment seems to be unavoidable feature of
R for any non-base-class object, certainly in S3 and AFAIK also in
S4. Base-R arrays \emph{can} do in-place modification, but there's
no safe general way in your own code for your own classes, not even
at the C level. I doubt R-core has much interest in changing this,
although the word \textquotedbl might\textquotedbl{} has been bandied
about for years. Packages like \texttt{data.table} get round it only
by defining new assignment operators, which I didn't want to do for
humble \texttt{offarray} objects.} of the entire offarray, which gets very costly for big offarrays.
Even if you only intend to run your R code once for \texttt{RTMB::MakeADFun},
normal for-loops can \emph{still} be waaaaay too slow. However, you
can \emph{completely solve the problem} by using \texttt{autoloop()}
to do (almost) all the loops for you, with \emph{minimal} cost in
comprehension. Cost in simple cases is about 2-3 times slower than
base-R, ie negligible penalty. Here's an example:

<<speed>>=
N <- 20 # array has 8000 elements (smallish)
system.time({
  xR <- array( 0, rep( N, 3));
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
    xR[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
})

system.time({
  xO <- offarray(0, dimseq=list( 1:N, 1:N, 1:N));
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
    xO[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
})

system.time( 
  xA <- autoloop( i=1:N, j=1:N, k=1:N, 
    1/(i+2*j+3*k))
)
N <- 100 # try 400 for a real test; then 8e6 elements and 512 MB
system.time( 
  xA <- autoloop( i=1:N, j=1:N, k=1:N, 1/(i+2*j+3*k))
)
## Don't even TRY the direct loop over offarrays!!!

system.time({
  xR <- array( 0, rep( N, 3));
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
    xR[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
})
@

Of course, sometimes you can't entirely avoid for-loops. A classic
example is time-stepping thru population-dynamics calculations over
the years. That's fine; by all means have one for-loop over years,
but just try to do all the work \emph{within} each year via one-or-more
\texttt{autoloop} calls. The Examples section in \texttt{?autoloop}
shows this.

\texttt{autoloop }will also do automatic \textquotedbl contractions\textquotedbl{}
for you, i.e. accumulating a sum over an index which does not appear
in the result. Matrix-multiplication is the most obvious example.
Of course, you would not normally use \texttt{autoloop} just for that,
but here's how you could. Notice also that \texttt{autoloop} can use
base-R arrays as well as offarrays :

<<matmul>>=
mm <- matrix( 1:6, 2, 3)
xx <- 4:6
mx <- autoloop( i=1:2, SUMOVER=list( j=1:3),
  mm[i,j] * xx[ j]
)
mx
mm %*% xx
@

Automatic contraction comes into its own with probability calculations
in population dynamics, and especially in close-kin mark-recapture,
when you might be dealing with a 7-dimensional set of indices that
you want to sum down to \textquotedbl just\textquotedbl{} 5; good
luck concocting a base-R expression for that!

You can have several statements within the body of an \texttt{autoloop},
wrapped inside \{\} and separated by semicolons. Some statements might
compute ephemeral quantities that are needed only for computing other
things, and that don't need to be kept afterwards. Other statements
can produce named variables that you do keep at the end. The examples
later on show all that.

\subsection{How does \texttt{autoloop} work?}

\texttt{?autoloop} 

If that's too much effort to read: \texttt{autoloop} is like base-R
\texttt{outer}, but generalized and betterized. Which should encourage
you to read \texttt{?autoloop}...

\section{Programming styles with \texttt{autoloop}}

\texttt{autoloop} auto-vectorizes your expression, and then evaluates
it over subsets of a few thousand index-combinations at a time. Therefore,
what it runs is not \emph{quite} what you wrote, but if you stick
to simple rules then you will never see the difference--- and if
you don't, it probably won't run at all. Basically, every statement
within your \texttt{autoloop} expression should result in a vector
of the same length. Even though array-accesses don't \emph{look} like
they will do that, the magic of \texttt{autoloop} ensures that they
generally do\footnote{Provided you stick to the rules. Are the rules documented? Not really,
though there are some in \texttt{?autoloop}. Just stick to them anyway,
is my advice.}.

Mathematical operations within an \texttt{autoloop} expression generally
work fine (though not matrix-multiply--- but that is simple to code
manually within the expression). The main \textquotedbl limitation\textquotedbl{}
is that you can't use \texttt{if}-statements\footnote{Nor \texttt{for/while/repeat/break/next}, but \texttt{if} is more
significant here.} The R function \texttt{ifelse()} can accomplish the same thing, but
be aware that \texttt{ifelse(cond,ex1,ex2)} always evaluates \emph{both}
\texttt{ex1} and \texttt{ex2} whether \texttt{cond} is TRUE or FALSE.
That can lead to OOB trouble if you naively try to access arrays,
which is usually what I use \texttt{autoloop} for. You can get around
OOBs succinctly with ``autoclamping'' syntax such as \texttt{x{[}\textasciitilde y-b{]}}
(the ``\texttt{\textasciitilde}'' means that the index will be
clamped to lie within the allowable range).

I'd like to put in a general Bayes-theorem example here, but I'd have
to think about it, and who's got time for that? So instead, here's
a no-thought example from Close-Kin Mark-Recapture. If you are currently
or imminently doing the CKMR workshop, maybe defer reading this until
Day 2! If not, you might not understand the background at all---
never mind :)

For a range of \textquotedbl adult\textquotedbl{} sampling years
Y and ages-at-sampling A, and \textquotedbl juvenile\textquotedbl{}
birth-years B, we consider a comparison between a potential parent
\textquotedbl adult\textquotedbl{} and potential offspring \textquotedbl juvenile\textquotedbl .
The adult is lethally caught in year Ya at age Aa, and the juvenile
in Yj at Aj. What is the demographic probability that the two will
be a Parent-Offspring Pair, aka \textquotedbl POP\textquotedbl ?
In simple cases, that probability is \textquotedbl just\textquotedbl{}
the adult's expected fecundity in the birth-year Bj (which will depend
on the adult's age at Bj, plus whether the adult would actually be
alive then), divided by the total fecundity across \emph{all} adults
in year B, which I've \textquotedbl pre-calculated\textquotedbl{}
for simplicity. Let's make all this sex-specific while we're at it,
because you always \emph{should} in CKMR. And we'll use \texttt{autoloop}.
Here's a naive example, showing off multi-statement code while we're
again at it:

<<ifelse-naive>>=
  SEXES <- c( "Female", "Male")
  adSAMPYEARS <- 1995:2000    # when our "adults" were sampled
  adAGES <- 6:9               # ... of adults (ie potential parents)
  juSAMPYEARS <- 1990:1999    # when our potential offspring were sampled
  juAGES <- 1:3               # ... and their range of ages at sampling
  juBYEARS <- (1990-3):(1999-1)  # implied range of juvenile birth-years

  # Any old values for fecundity-at-age...
  fec_SA <- autoloop( S=SEXES, A=adAGES, log( A))
  
  # Any old values for total fecundity over the years... say from 1e6 typical age-7 adults
  totfec_SB <- autoloop( S=SEXES, B=juBYEARS, 1e6 * fec_SA[ S, 7])

  try( Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {  # curly required coz multiple statements
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <- ifelse( Ya >= Bj,         # was adult still alive then?
      fec_SA[ Sa, Aa_at_Bj] / totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj],  # yes: competing against other adults
      0)                            # no: dead already
    return( Pr)
   }))
@

Well, that didn't work, but at least the error message gives you a
chance to figure out why. The problem is with accessing \texttt{fec\_SA{[}Sa,Aa\_at\_Bj{]}}
and clearly \texttt{Sa} won't be the cause, so it must be \texttt{Aa\_at\_Bj}
. The error message doesn't show you \texttt{Aa\_at\_Bj} directly\footnote{While producing this example, I have started to think that the error
message should include \emph{all} available variables (including ones
just calculated), not just the indices, because that might clarify
things a lot! Next version...}, because it's calculated only within the expression, but we can see
where it came from. With \texttt{Ya=1995,Aa=9,Yj=1992,Aj=1} we would
have \texttt{Bj=1992-1=1991} and \texttt{Ya-Bj=1995-1991=4} and \texttt{Aa\_at\_Bj=9-4=5}.
But the range of adult ages, \texttt{adAGES}, which indexes \texttt{fec\_SA},
is only 6:9. So the real problem is that our \textquotedbl adult\textquotedbl{}
wasn't adult back then; s/he was too young. (The calculation could
even have led to a negative age for the adult, if the juvenile was
born before s/he was!) While we're at it, there is also the possibility
of exceeding the maximum adult age--- it won't be the case in the
samples, because sampling kills the adults, but if we compare an adult
to a juvenile born \emph{after} the adult capture, then er \emph{projected}
age could be OOB. Let's fix that quickly:

<<ifelse-still-naive>>=
  try( Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <- ifelse( 
        (Ya >= Bj) &                   # too dead; note single ampersand coz vector
        (Aa_at_Bj >= min( adAGES)) &   # too young
        (Aa_at_Bj <= max( adAGES)),    # too old in future (and will actually be already dead!)
      fec_SA[ Sa, Aa_at_Bj] / totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj],  # yes: competing
      0)                             # no: not around
    return( Pr)
   }))
@

Same goddamn problem. The logic is fine now, but the problem is that
\texttt{ifelse} still evaluates both the \textquotedbl yes\textquotedbl{}
and \textquotedbl no\textquotedbl{} cases every time. So what we
have to do, is to fix up the array access to \emph{never} OOB, by
tweaking the index; the \texttt{ifelse(...,0)} will give the right
answer overall in such cases anyway, and we won't offend the OOB gods.
There are many ways to code that, but the easiest is perhaps to pipe
thru the \texttt{mvbutils::clamp} function, which restricts its input
to a range:

<<ifelse-wise>>=
  # No try() needed this time!
  Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <- ifelse( 
        (Ya >= Bj) &                  # too dead
        (Aa_at_Bj >= min( adAGES)) &  # too young
        (Aa_at_Bj <= max( adAGES)),   # too old
      fec_SA[ Sa, Aa_at_Bj |> clamp( adAGES)] /
        totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj],          # yes, competing
      0)                             # not around
    return( Pr)
   })
@

Over time, I have come to feel that \texttt{ifelse} offers little
by way of clarity and speed in contexts like this. Instead, I take
advantage of \textquotedbl multiplication by 0\textquotedbl{} and
R's automatic type-casting of logicals to numerics, like so:

<<ifelse-gone>>=
  Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <-
      (Ya >= Bj) *                   # if FALSE, entire expression will give 0
      (Aa_at_Bj >= min( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      (Aa_at_Bj <= max( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      fec_SA[ Sa, Aa_at_Bj |> clamp( adAGES)] /
        totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj]  

    return( Pr)
   })
@

Lovely! And while I'm still at it, I'll illustrate another \texttt{autoloop}
feature. First, you can return multiple arguments--- they will each
be an \texttt{offarray} with the same dimensions, and they'll will
be put together into a named list. Second, you can use \texttt{mvbutils::extract.named}
to immediately turn those list elements into actual variables, in
the place where you do the call. The demonstration will compute the
expected number of kin-pairs by covariate category, based on the relevant
numbers of samples:

<<twofer>>=
  Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <-
      (Ya >= Bj) *                   # if FALSE, entire expression will give 0
      (Aa_at_Bj >= min( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      (Aa_at_Bj <= max( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      fec_SA[ Sa, Aa_at_Bj |> clamp( adAGES)] /
        totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj]  

    return( Pr)
   })
@

Well, that's pretty good, but TBF the use of \texttt{clamp} is ugly.
We can improve that (in v2.0.74+) with autoclamping:

<<autoclamp>>=
  Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <-
      (Ya >= Bj) *                   # if FALSE, entire expression will give 0
      (Aa_at_Bj >= min( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      (Aa_at_Bj <= max( adAGES)) *   # ditto
      fec_SA[ Sa, ~Aa_at_Bj] /       # "~" clamps that index to allowable range
        totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj]  

    return( Pr)
   })
@

And that's not bad, but still a bit lengthy--- perhaps cozza the
two explict tests on \texttt{Aa\_at\_Bj}. The ``\textasciitilde ''
prevents OOB lookups based on ``illegal'' \texttt{Aa\_at\_Bj}, but
we'd be happy with a zero in such cases. A really fancy way to do
that, is to set up a zero-padded version of \texttt{fec\_SA} so that
the autoclamp will automatically zero any OOB attempts. This isn't
always what you want\footnote{In real popdyn, you might not want to zero-pad beyond the max age;
it might be a plus-group where you are fine just to use the same value
for any greater age.}, but here it's quite nice:

<<zero-pad-autoclamp>>=
  fec_SA_zp <- autoloop( S=SEXES, A=(min(adAGES)-1):(max(adAGES)+1), 0)
  fec_SA_zp[,adAGES] <- fec_SA

  Pr_POP_SYAYA <- autoloop( 
      Sa=SEXES, Ya=adSAMPYEARS, Aa=adAGES, 
      Yj=juSAMPYEARS, Aj=juAGES, {
    Bj <- Yj - Aj;                  # birth of Our juvenile
    Aa_at_Bj <- Aa - (Ya - Bj);     # how old was Our adult then?
    Pr <-
      (Ya >= Bj) *                   # if FALSE, entire expression will give 0
      fec_SA_zp[ Sa, ~Aa_at_Bj] /       # "~" clamps that index to allowable range
        totfec_SB[ Sa, Bj]  

    return( Pr)
   })
@

\subsection{Using \texttt{autobayes}}

I often find myself using \texttt{autoloop} for discrete Bayes calculations,
e.g. to get 
\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}\left[X|Y,Z\right]=\frac{\mathbb{P}\left[X,Y|Z\right]}{\mathbb{P}\left[Y|Z\right]}
\end{gather*}
where the denominator is given by summing the numerator over all values
of $X$. With pre-v2.0.74 \texttt{offarray}, this requires 3 operations:
\begin{enumerate}
\item calculate an ``unscaled'' joint distribution $\mathbb{P}\left[X,Y|Z\right]$
via \texttt{autoloop()}
\item use \texttt{sumover(...,'X')} to get $\mathbb{P}\left[Y,Z\right]$
\item use \texttt{autoloop()} again to divide the joint distribution by
the denominator, for each value of $Y$ and $Z$.
\end{enumerate}
This can now be done in one step just by calling \texttt{autobayes()}
instead of \texttt{autoloop()} in step 1, specifying \texttt{RESPONSES=list(
X=<xrange>)} and \texttt{CONDITIONERS=list(Y=<yrange>,Z=<zrange>)}.
In fact, \texttt{autobayes()} is convenient whenever you want to normalize
something to sum-to-1; see the Examples section of \texttt{?autoloop}.

\section{With package \texttt{RTMB}}

This isn't the right place to document the \texttt{RTMB} link! There
should be a separate demo, probably in package \texttt{offartmb}.
Anyway, the general point is: you can write a function using \texttt{offarray}
and \texttt{autoloop}, and with minimal care you can evaluate it either
directly in R, or via \texttt{RTMB::MakeADFun}, which creates a C-level
version of your function \emph{and }its derivative. That means it
will run really fast, especially in the context of fitting to data.
Plus, \texttt{RTMB} can automatically do Laplace approximation to
integrate out random effects, etc etc. WOW!

What you need to do:
\begin{itemize}
\item Load the package \texttt{offartmb}, after\footnote{I have added a hack that tries to make the loading-order irrelevant.
\textquotedbl Tries\textquotedbl .} loading \texttt{offarray} and \texttt{RTMB}.
\item Wrap the body of your \textquotedbl objective function\textquotedbl{}
in a call to \texttt{reclasso}, as shown below. This will have \emph{no
effect} if you are running the function directly in R, but will also
make it work nicely with \texttt{RTMB::MakeADFun}. It won't work if
you don't.
\item If you want to store variables calculated inside your objective function,
so they are available afterwards, name them in a call to \texttt{REPORTO()}
somewhere (like the C macro \texttt{REPORT()} in good old TMB).
\item Avoid comparison-operations in your code, eg \textquotedbl ==\textquotedbl ,
\textquotedbl >\textquotedbl . RTMB hates them. You might be able
to get away with them if they only apply to \textquotedbl data\textquotedbl ,
but in that case you are better off doing them beforehand and storing
the results as additional data objects.
\item Good practice is to put the data needed by your function into a separate
\texttt{environment}, and attach that environment to your function.
The things from \texttt{REPORTO()} will be stored there too. This
is a general principle well worth learning about in R--- not specific
to \texttt{RTMB}, but perhaps particularly relevant there. The CKMR
course examples all do it.
\end{itemize}
An example, but not one to run:

<<rtmb>>=
if( FALSE){ # don't wanna run this!
  library( offartmb)

  myobjfun <- function( pars) reclasso( by=pars, { # magic line
      Ey <- X %% pars
      resid <- y-Ey
      REPORTO( Ey, resid) # two at once; could separate
    return( sum( sqr( y-Ey)))
    })

  X <- cbind( 1, 1:5) # regression
  y <- c( 5, 3, 4, 1, 2)
  pp0 <- 1:2

  # Base-R
  myobjfun( pp0)
  numvbderiv( myobjfun, pp0) # numerical deriv (rough)

  # RTMB
  ooo <- RTMB::MakeADFun( myobjfun, pp0)
  ooo$fn( pp0)
  ooo$gr( pp0)
  # ... and they all lived happily ever after
}
@

As yet, I \emph{haven't} tested\slash implemented the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item several named parameter subsets (it's all just one vector at present,
to be unpacked inside your function--- coz that's what \texttt{optim/nlminb}
expects of a base-R function); {[}Surely I must have tested this by
now???{]}
\item To-do: auto-choose a factorizable blocksize when the \texttt{SUMOVER
}arg is used, to allow fastest \texttt{.colSums} internally etc. 
\item there's no simulation or OSA capability yet.
\end{itemize}

\end{document}
